      "source": [
        "# Parallel / Swarm / Networked Architectures Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` implements a swarm of 4 worker threads pulling from a shared `queue.Queue`. Tasks have variable durations (some fast, some slow). The demo contrasts:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Sequential baseline:** one worker processes all tasks serially."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Fixed assignment:** each task pre-assigned to a specific worker (supervisor-style)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Swarm:** workers pull from a shared queue."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Swarm balances load automatically; fixed assignment leaves fast workers idle when their assigned task is slow."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Run:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "python3 code/main.py"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Output shows per-worker task counts (swarm distributes unevenly but optimally) and wall-clock times."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Run `code/main.py`. How much faster is swarm than sequential on the variable-duration workload? How much faster than fixed assignment?\n2. Add a priority queue variant (use `queue.PriorityQueue`). Assign priority by task \"importance\" field. Observe whether low-priority tasks ever starve under continuous load.\n3. Implement a hot-spot detector: log when any worker processes 3\u00d7 more tasks than the slowest worker. What does that indicate about task-duration distribution?\n4. Read the Matrix paper (arXiv:2511.21686) abstract and Section 3. Identify one specific tradeoff Matrix accepts (scalability gain) and one it gives up (traceability, determinism).\n5. Convert the swarm demo to use a `queue.Queue` of (task_type, payload) tuples, with workers subscribing only to specific types. What routing rules make sense when tasks are heterogeneous?"
      ]
    }
  ],
